    questionText: "It is established that an electric current through a metallic conductor produces a magnetic field around it. Is there a similar magnetic field produced around a thin beam of moving (i) alpha particles, (ii) neutrons? Justify your answer.",
    answer: "An electric current is essentially a flow of electric charge; any moving charge produces a magnetic field around it. (i) Alpha particles are positively charged (charge +2e). A beam of moving alpha particles therefore constitutes a current and DOES produce a magnetic field around it. (ii) Neutrons are electrically neutral (charge = 0). A beam of moving neutrons does not constitute an electric current and hence produces NO magnetic field around it.",
